        <h2 id="how-it-works">How Semaglutide Works</h2>
        <p>Semaglutide works through multiple mechanisms simultaneously:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Brain signaling</strong> — Acts on appetite-regulating centres in the hypothalamus, reducing hunger and increasing satiety.</li>
          <li><strong>Gastric motility</strong> — Slows stomach emptying, keeping you full longer after meals.</li>
          <li><strong>Insulin regulation</strong> — Stimulates glucose-dependent insulin secretion, reducing hypoglycaemia risk.</li>
          <li><strong>Glucagon suppression</strong> — Reduces glucagon secretion, helping lower blood sugar.</li>
          <li><strong>"Food noise" reduction</strong> — Many patients describe a dramatic reduction in constant food-related thoughts and cravings.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="callout"><strong>Why weekly?</strong> Natural GLP-1 has a half-life of just 2 minutes. Semaglutide is chemically modified (94% structurally similar to human GLP-1) to bind to albumin in your blood, giving it a half-life of approximately 7 days.</div>

        <h2 id="clinical-results">Clinical Trial Results</h2>
        <h3>STEP Trials (Weight Management)</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>STEP 1:</strong> Average weight loss of 14.9% over 68 weeks vs. 2.4% with placebo.</li>
          <li><strong>STEP 2</strong> (type 2 diabetes): Average weight loss of 9.6%.</li>
          <li><strong>STEP 3</strong> (with behavioural therapy): Average weight loss of 16%.</li>
          <li><strong>STEP 5</strong> (2-year study): ~15% weight loss sustained over 2 years of continued treatment.</li>
        </ul>
        <h3>SELECT Trial (Cardiovascular)</h3>
        <p>The landmark SELECT trial demonstrated semaglutide reduced major adverse cardiovascular events by 20% in overweight or obese adults with established cardiovascular disease — even without diabetes. This shifted semaglutide from "weight loss drug" to a medication with proven heart benefits.</p>
        <div class="callout"><strong>Key finding:</strong> About one-third of STEP trial participants lost 20%+ of their body weight — results previously only achievable through bariatric surgery.</div>

