        <h2 id="side-effects">Side Effects</h2>
        <h3>Very Common (&gt;10% of patients)</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Nausea</strong> (44%) — worst during dose escalations</li>
          <li><strong>Diarrhoea</strong> (30%)</li>
          <li><strong>Vomiting</strong> (24%)</li>
          <li><strong>Constipation</strong> (24%)</li>
          <li><strong>Abdominal pain</strong> (20%)</li>
        </ul>
        <h3>Common (1–10%)</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Headache, fatigue, dizziness</li>
          <li>Injection site reactions</li>
          <li>Gastroesophageal reflux</li>
          <li>Hair thinning (related to rapid weight loss)</li>
        </ul>
        <h3>Serious (Rare)</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Pancreatitis</li>
          <li>Gallbladder disease</li>
          <li>Thyroid C-cell tumours (boxed warning based on animal studies)</li>
          <li>Acute kidney injury (usually from dehydration)</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="callout"><strong>Pro tip:</strong> Smaller, more frequent meals, avoiding fatty foods, and staying well hydrated can significantly reduce GI side effects. Most peak in the first 2–3 weeks of each dose increase then subside.</div>

        <h2 id="cost">Cost &amp; Insurance</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Ozempic:</strong> ~$900–$1,000/month (list price)</li>
          <li><strong>Wegovy:</strong> ~$1,300–$1,400/month (list price)</li>
          <li><strong>Rybelsus:</strong> ~$900–$1,000/month (list price)</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Insurance coverage varies widely. Manufacturer savings cards can reduce copays to $0–$25/month for commercially insured patients. Compounded semaglutide is available from some pharmacies at $150–$500/month, though not FDA-approved.</p>

